{"accessed_at":"2026-11-08T05:08:50.461Z","response":"laminarin (CHEBI:6364) (https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI%3A6364 ; rewritten summary of https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:6364)\nciteturn28view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:6364\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:6364; Total lines: 87\nL0:   * 【0†Advanced Search】\nL1:   * 【1†Submit】\nL2:   * 【2†Downloads】\nL3:   * 【3†Tools】\nL4:   * 【4†Docs】\nL5:   * 【5†About】\nL6:   * 【6†Contact】\nL7: \nL8: 【7†EMBL-EBI†ebi.ac.uk】 | 【8†Chemical Biology Resources】 | 【9†ChEBI】\nL9: \nL10: Search[Input: Enter your search term]\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: \nL14: Example searches: 【10†iron*】, 【11†InChI=1S/CH4O/c1-2/h2H,1H3】, 【12†caffeine】 | 【0†Advanced Search】\nL15: ## CHEBI:6364 - laminarin\nL16: \nL17: ChEBI ID  | CHEBI:6364\nL18: ChEBI Name  | laminarin\nL19: Stars  |\nL20: Definition  | A polysaccharide composed of β-(1→3)-linked glucose residues containing sporadic β-(1→6)-linkages as branch points or inter-residue linkages and 2-3% D-mannitol at some reducing termini.\nL21: Last Modified  | 8 December 2014\nL22: \nL23: On this page\nL24: \nL25:   * 【13†Intro】\nL26:   * 【14†Species of Metabolite】\nL27:   * 【15†Roles Classification】\nL28:   * 【16†Ontology】\nL29:   * 【17†Names】\nL30:   * 【18†Manual Xrefs】\nL31:   * 【19†Registry Numbers】\nL32:   * 【20†Citations】\nL33: Formula  | C12H22O11(C6H10O5)n\nL34: \nL35: Wikipedia\nL36: ---\nL37: \nL38: Species of Metabolite  | Component  | Source  | Comments\nL39: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL40: Eisenia bicyclis (【21†ncbitaxon:6395†bioregistry.io】)  | -  | PubMed (【22†24274485†europepmc.org】)  |\nL41: Laminaria digitata (【23†ncbitaxon:80365†bioregistry.io】)  | -  | PubMed (【24†25443973†europepmc.org】)  |\nL42: Sargassum fusiforme (【25†ncbitaxon:590727†bioregistry.io】)  | -  | PubMed (【26†24413558†europepmc.org】)  |\nL43: \nL44: Roles Classification\nL45: ---\nL46: 【27†Biological Roles:】  | 【28†marine metabolite】  Any metabolite produced during a metabolic reaction in marine macro- and microorganisms.【29†immunological adjuvant】  A substance that augments, stimulates, activates, potentiates, or modulates the immune response at either the cellular or humoral level. A classical agent (Freund's adjuvant, BCG, Corynebacterium parvum, et al.) contains bacterial antigens. It could also be endogenous (e.g., histamine, interferon, transfer factor, tuftsin, interleukin-1).\nL47: Its mode of action is either non-specific, resulting in increased immune responsiveness to a wide variety of antigens, or antigen-specific, i.e., affecting a restricted type of immune response to a narrow group of antigens. The therapeutic efficacy is related to its antigen-specific immunoadjuvanticity.【30†apoptosis inducer】  Any substance that induces the process of apoptosis (programmed cell death) in multi-celled organisms.\nL48: 【31†Applications:】  | 【29†immunological adjuvant】  A substance that augments, stimulates, activates, potentiates, or modulates the immune response at either the cellular or humoral level. A classical agent (Freund's adjuvant, BCG, Corynebacterium parvum, et al.) contains bacterial antigens. It could also be endogenous (e.g., histamine, interferon, transfer factor, tuftsin, interleukin-1).\nL49: Its mode of action is either non-specific, resulting in increased immune responsiveness to a wide variety of antigens, or antigen-specific, i.e., affecting a restricted type of immune response to a narrow group of antigens.\nL50: The therapeutic efficacy is related to its antigen-specific immunoadjuvanticity.【32†animal growth promotant】  Substances that are administered to farmed animals to improve productivity by promoting weight gain, increasing muscle mass, limiting fat deposition, reducing feed consumption, and reducing waste production.\nL51: ChEBI Ontology\nL52: ---\nL53: \nL54: Text\nL55: \nL56: Tree View\nL57: \nL58: Graph View\nL59: \nL60: Outgoing Relation(s)\nL61: laminarin (【33†CHEBI:6364】) has role animal growth promotant (【32†CHEBI:82655】)\nL62: laminarin (【33†CHEBI:6364】) has role apoptosis inducer (【30†CHEBI:68495】)\nL63: laminarin (【33†CHEBI:6364】) has role immunological adjuvant (【29†CHEBI:50847】)\nL64: laminarin (【33†CHEBI:6364】) has role marine metabolite (【28†CHEBI:76507】)\nL65: laminarin (【33†CHEBI:6364】) is a polysaccharide (【34†CHEBI:18154】)\nL66: Synonym s   | Source\nL67: --- | ---\nL68: algal β-1,3-glucan  | ChEBI\nL69: Laminaran  | KEGG COMPOUND\nL70: Laminarin  | KEGG COMPOUND\nL71: \nL72: Manual Xrefs  | Databases\nL73: --- | ---\nL74: 【35†C00771†bioregistry.io】  | KEGG COMPOUND\nL75: 【36†CPD-3602†bioregistry.io】  | MetaCyc\nL76: 【37†Laminarin†bioregistry.io】  | Wikipedia\nL77: \nL78: Registry Numbers  | Sources\nL79: --- | ---\nL80: Reaxys:10330714  | Reaxys\nL81: Reaxys:8187226  | Reaxys\nL82: CAS:【38†9008-22-4†bioregistry.io】  | ChemIDplus\nL83: CAS:9008-22-4  | KEGG COMPOUND\nL84: \nL85: Citations\nL86: ---\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npyrroloquinoline quinone (CHEBI:18315) (https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI%3A18315 ; rewritten summary of https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:18315)\nciteturn28view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:18315\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:18315; Total lines: 108\nL0:   * 【0†Advanced Search】\nL1:   * 【1†Submit】\nL2:   * 【2†Downloads】\nL3:   * 【3†Tools】\nL4:   * 【4†Docs】\nL5:   * 【5†About】\nL6:   * 【6†Contact】\nL7: \nL8: 【7†EMBL-EBI†ebi.ac.uk】 | 【8†Chemical Biology Resources】 | 【9†ChEBI】\nL9: \nL10: Search[Input: Enter your search term]\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: \nL14: Example searches: 【10†iron*】, 【11†InChI=1S/CH4O/c1-2/h2H,1H3】, 【12†caffeine】 | 【0†Advanced Search】\nL15: ## CHEBI:18315 - pyrroloquinoline quinone\nL16: \nL17: 【13†Image: Default Structure】\nL18: \nL19: Take structure to advanced search\nL20: \nL21: ChEBI ID  | CHEBI:18315\nL22: ChEBI Name  | pyrroloquinoline quinone\nL23: Stars  |\nL24: Definition  | A pyrroloquinoline having oxo groups at the 4- and 5-positions and carboxy groups at the 2-, 7- and 9-positions.\nL25: Secondary ChEBI ID s  | CHEBI:7881, CHEBI:14986, CHEBI:26460, CHEBI:45251, CHEBI:49082\nL26: Last Modified  | 4 August 2021\nL27: Submitter  | Marcus Ennis\nL28: Downloads  | 【14†Molfile】\nL29: \nL30: On this page\nL31:   * 【15†Intro】\nL32:   * 【16†Roles Classification】\nL33:   * 【17†Ontology】\nL34:   * 【18†Names】\nL35:   * 【19†Manual Xrefs】\nL36:   * 【20†Registry Numbers】\nL37:   * 【21†Citations】\nL38: \nL39: Formula  | C14H6N2O8\nL40: Net Charge  | 0\nL41: Average Mass  | 330.208\nL42: Monoisotopic Mass  | 330.01242\nL43: SMILES  | O=C(O)c1cc(C(=O)O)c2c(n1)C(=O)C(=O)c1cc(C(=O)O)nc1-2\nL44: InChI  | InChI=1S/C14H6N2O8/c17-10-4-2-6(14(23)24)15-8(4)7-3(12(19)20)1-5(13(21)22)16-9(7)11(10)18/h1-2,15H,(H,19,20)(H,21,22)(H,23,24)\nL45: InChIKey  | MMXZSJMASHPLLR-UHFFFAOYSA-N\nL46: \nL47: Wikipedia\nL48: ---\nL49: Roles Classification\nL50: ---\nL51: 【22†Chemical Role s:】  | 【23†antioxidant】  A substance that opposes oxidation or inhibits reactions brought about by dioxygen or peroxides.【24†Bronsted acid】  A molecular entity capable of donating a hydron to an acceptor (Brønsted base).\nL52: 【25†Biological Role s:】  | 【26†water-soluble vitamin (role)】  Any vitamin that dissolves in water and readily absorbed into tissues for immediate use. Unlike the fat-soluble vitamins, they are not stored in the body and need to be replenished regularly in the diet and will rarely accumulate to toxic levels since they are quickly excreted from the body via urine.【27†cofactor】  An organic molecule or ion (usually a metal ion) that is required by an enzyme for its activity.\nL53: It may be attached either loosely (coenzyme) or tightly (prosthetic group).【27†cofactor】  An organic molecule or ion (usually a metal ion) that is required by an enzyme for its activity. It may be attached either loosely (coenzyme) or tightly (prosthetic group).\nL54: 【28†Application:】  | 【29†anti-inflammatory agent】  Any compound that has anti-inflammatory effects.\nL55: ChEBI Ontology\nL56: ---\nL57: \nL58: Text\nL59: \nL60: Tree View\nL61: \nL62: Graph View\nL63: Outgoing Relation(s)\nL64: pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】) has role anti-inflammatory agent (【29†CHEBI:67079】)\nL65: pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】) has role antioxidant (【23†CHEBI:22586】)\nL66: pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】) has role cofactor (【27†CHEBI:23357】)\nL67: pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】) has role water-soluble vitamin (role) (【26†CHEBI:27314】)\nL68: pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】) is a orthoquinones (【31†CHEBI:25622】)\nL69: pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】) is a pyrroloquinoline cofactor (【32†CHEBI:26461】)\nL70: pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】) is a tricarboxylic acid (【33†CHEBI:27093】)\nL71: pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】) is conjugate acid of pyrroloquinoline quinone(3−) (【34†CHEBI:58442】)\nL72: Incoming Relation(s)\nL73: pyrroloquinoline quinone(3−) (【34†CHEBI:58442】) is conjugate base of pyrroloquinoline quinone (【30†CHEBI:18315】)\nL74: IUPAC Name\nL75: ---\nL76: 4,5-dioxo-4,5-dihydro-1H-pyrrolo[2,3-f]quinoline-2,7,9-tricarboxylic acid\nL77: \nL78: Synonym s   | Source\nL79: --- | ---\nL80: 2,4,6-tricarboxylic-pyrrolo[2,3-5,6]quinoline 8,9-quinone  | RESID\nL81: 2,7,9-tricarboxy-1H-pyrrolo(2,3-f)quinoline-4,5-dione  | ChemIDplus\nL82: 4,5-dihydro-4,5-dioxo-1H-pyrrolo[2,3-5,6]quinoline-2,7,9-tricarboxylic acid  | RESID\nL83: 4,5-Dioxo-4,5-dihydro-1H-pyrrolo[2,3-f]quinoline-2,7,9-tricarboxylate  | KEGG COMPOUND\nL84: coenzyme PQQ  | ChemIDplus\nL85: methoxatin  | ChemIDplus\nL86: \nL87: [Button: Show All]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nL -Asparagine monohydrate (A8381) - Product Information Sheet (https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/deepweb/assets/sigmaaldrich/product/documents/313/204/a8381pis.pdf)\nciteturn28view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 2; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/deepweb/assets/sigmaaldrich/product/documents/313/204/a8381pis.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 87\nL0@P0: L-Asparagine monohydrate\nL1@P0: Product Number A 8381\nL2@P0: Store at Room Temperature\nL3@P0: Product Description\nL4@P0: Molecular Formula: C4H8N2O3 • H2O\nL5@P0: Molecular Weight: 150.1\nL6@P0: CAS Number: 5794-13-8\nL7@P0: pKa: 2.14 (-COOH), 8.72 (-NH2)\nL8@P0: 1\nL9@P0: pI: 5.431\nL10@P0: Specific Rotation: +32.6°\nL11@P0: (10 mg/ml, 0.1 M HCl, 20 °C)\nL12@P0: Melting Point: 234-235 °C2\nL13@P0: Synonyms: (S)-2-aminosuccinic acid 4-amide,\nL14@P0: L-aspartic acid 4-amide, α-aminoauccinamic acid,\nL15@P0: aspartic acid β-amide2\nL16@P0: The amino acid L-asparagine is a structural analog of\nL17@P0: L-aspartic acid, where the side chain of the carboxylic\nL18@P0: acid moiety is amidated, to give a terminal amine\nL19@P0: group. This renders L-asparagine neutral at\nL20@P0: physiological pH. The amide group of asparagine is\nL21@P0: derived from glutamate, in the reaction of aspartate\nL22@P0: and glutamine in the presence of ATP to yield\nL23@P0: asparagine and glutamate.3\nL24@P0:  In vivo, asparagine is\nL25@P0: hydrolyzed to aspartate and NH4\nL26@P0: +\nL27@P0:  by asparaginase.4\nL28@P0: Asparagine is also an important amino acid in\nL29@P0: glycopeptide bonds, via N-glycosyl linkages to the\nL30@P0: sugar rings.3\nL31@P0: L-Asparagine is used in cell culture media and is a\nL32@P0: component of MEM non-essential amino acids solution\nL33@P0: (Product No. M 7145). L-Asparagine has been shown\nL34@P0: to enhance ornithine decarboxylase activity in cultured\nL35@P0: human colon adenocarcinoma Caco-2 cells and in\nL36@P0: cultured IEC-6 intestinal epithelial cells.5,6 Spore\nL37@P0: germination in Bacillus subtilis has been increased in\nL38@P0: the presence of L-asparagine.7\nL39@P0: An isoxazoline RGD mimic platelet GPIIb/IIIa\nL40@P0: antagonist has been prepared by chiral synthesis with\nL41@P0: L-asparagine as a starting material.8\nL42@P0:  L-Asparagine\nL43@P0: has been utilized in the synthesis of 4-azalysine\nL44@P0: building blocks for application to combinatorial\nL45@P0: chemistry.9\nL46@P0: \nL47@P0: Precautions and Disclaimer\nL48@P0: For Laboratory Use Only. Not for drug, household or\nL49@P0: other uses.\nL50@P0: Preparation Instructions\nL51@P0: This product is soluble in water (50 mg/ml), with heat\nL52@P0: as needed, yielding a clear, colorless solution.\nL53@P0: References\nL54@P0: 1. Molecular Biology LabFax, Brown, T. A., ed., BIOS\nL55@P0: Scientific Publishers (Oxford, UK: 1991), p. 29.\nL56@P0: 2. The Merck Index, 12th ed., Entry# 872.\nL57@P0: 3. Textbook of Biochemistry with Clinical\nL58@P0: Correlations, 5th ed., Devlin, T. M., ed., Wiley-Liss\nL59@P0: (New York, NY: 2002), pp. 97, 680-681, 785-786.\nL60@P0: 4. Biochemistry, 3rd ed., Stryer, L., W. H. Freeman\nL61@P0: (New York, NY: 1988), pp. 18, 504.\nL62@P0: 5. Chabanon, H., et al., Increased translation\nL63@P0: efficiency and antizyme-dependent stabilization of\nL64@P0: ornithine decarboxylase in amino acid\u0002supplemented human colon adenocarcinoma\nL65@P0: cells, Caco-2. Biochem. J., 348(Pt 2), 401-408\nL66@P0: (2000).\nL67@P0: 6. Ray, R. M., et al., Interaction of asparagine and\nL68@P0: EGF in the regulation of ornithine decarboxylase in\nL69@P0: IEC-6 cells. Am. J. Physiol., 276(3 Pt 1), G773-\nL70@P0: 780.\nL71@P0: 7. Cabrera-Martinez, R. M., et al., Effects of\nL72@P0: overexpression of nutrient receptors on\nL73@P0: germination of spores of Bacillus subtilis. J.\nL74@P0-1: Bacteriol., 185(8), 2457-2464 (2003). 8. Zhang L. H., et al., The Enantiospecific synthesis\nL75@P1: of an isoxazoline. A RGD mimic platelet GPIIb/IIIa\nL76@P1: antagonist. J. Org. Chem., 62(8), 2466-2470\nL77@P1: (1997).\nL78@P1: 9. Chhabra, S. R., et al., Homochiral 4-azalysine\nL79@P1: building blocks: syntheses and applications in\nL80@P1: solid-phase chemistry. J. Org. Chem., 67(12),\nL81@P1: 4017-4029 (2002).\nL82@P1: GCY/RXR 8/03\nL83@P1: Sigma brand products are sold through Sigma-Aldrich, Inc.\nL84@P1: Sigma-Aldrich, Inc. warrants that its products conform to the information contained in this and other Sigma-Aldrich publications. Purchaser\nL85@P1: must determine the suitability of the product(s) for their particular use. Additional terms and conditions may apply. Please see reverse side of\nL86@P1: the invoice or packing slip.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStudies on Primocarcin. II Isolation and Properties of Primocarcin (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/antibioticsa/15/2/15_77/_article/-char/en)\nciteturn28view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/antibioticsa/15/2/15_77/_article/-char/en\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 110\nL0: The Journal of Antibiotics, Series A\nL1: \nL2: Online ISSN : 2435-5135\nL3: Print ISSN : 0368-1173\nL4: ISSN-L : 0368-1173\nL5: \nL6:   * 【0†Journal home】\nL7:   * 【1†All issues】\nL8:   * 【2†About the journal】\nL9: \nL10: Original Articles\nL11: \nL12: Studies on Primocarcin. II Isolation and Properties of Primocarcin\nL13: \nL14: 【3†Kiyoshi Isono】, 【4†Saburō Suzuki】\nL15: \nL16: Author information\nL17: \nL18:   * Kiyoshi Isono\nL19: \nL20: Antibiotic Research Laboratory, The Institute of Physical & Chemical Research\nL21: \nL22:   * Saburō Suzuki\nL23: Antibiotic Research Laboratory, The Institute of Physical & Chemical Research\nL24: #### Corresponding author\nL25: \nL26: 【5†Image: ORCID】\nL27: \nL28: JOURNAL FREE ACCESS\nL29: \nL30: 1962 Volume 15 Issue 2 Pages 77-79\nL31: \nL32: DOI 【6†https://doi.org/10.11554/antibioticsa.15.2_77†doi.org】\nL33: \nL34: Details\nL35: \nL36:   * Published: 1962 Received: June 14, 1961 Available on J-STAGE: July 07, 2020 Accepted: - Advance online publication: - Revised: -\nL37: \nL38: 【7†Download PDF (1226K)】\nL39: \nL40: Download citation 【8†RIS】\nL41: \nL42: (compatible with EndNote, Reference Manager, ProCite, RefWorks)\nL43: \nL44: 【9†BIB TEX】\nL45: \nL46: (compatible with BibDesk, LaTeX)\nL47: \nL48: Text\nL49: \nL50: 【10†How to download citation】\nL51: 【11†Contact us】\nL52: \nL53: Article overview\nL54: \nL55:   * 【12†Abstract】\nL56:   * 【13†References (3)】\nL57:   * 【14†Content from these authors】\nL58: \nL59: Share\nL60: \nL61: Abstract\nL62: In the course of our antitumor antibiotic screen program using Ehrlich ascites carcinoma, a new antibiotic primocarcin was isolated in crystalline from the culture filtrate of an actinomycete designated as Nocardia fukayae^{1)}. The antibiotic is a water-soluble neutral compound having the formula C_{8}H_{12}N_{2}O_{3} and shows marked tumor-inhibitory effects against ascites type of Ehrlich carzinoma and Crocker sarcoma 180.\nL63: This paper deals with fermentation and isolation procefures as well as the chemical and biological properties of the antibiotic.\nL64: [Input]\nL65: \nL66: References (3)\nL67: \nL68: Related articles (0)\nL69: \nL70: Figures (0)\nL71: \nL72: 【15†Image】 Content from these authors\nL73: \nL74: Supplementary material (0)\nL75: \nL76: Result List ()\nL77: \nL78: Cited by (0)\nL79: \nL80: © 1962 JAPAN ANTIBIOTICS RESEARCH ASSOCIATION\nL81: \nL82: 【16†Previous article】 【17†Next article】\nL83: \nL84: Favorites & Alerts\nL85: \nL86:   * Add to favorites\nL87:   * Additional info alert\nL88:   * Citation alert\nL89:   * Authentication alert\nL90: \nL91: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL92: \nL93: 【15†Image】\nL94: \nL95: Related articles\nL96: \nL97: Recently viewed articles\nL98: \nL99: Successor\nL100: \nL101: 【18†The Journal of Antibiotics】\nL102: \nL103: Share this page\nL104: 【19†Top】\nL105: ### Register with J-STAGE for free!\nL106: \nL107: 【20†Register】\nL108: \nL109: Already have an account? Sign in 【21†here】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://repository.tudelft.nl/file/File_0700877a-931e-411b-b0c3-f612deb5e3b7?preview=1)\nciteturn28view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://repository.tudelft.nl/file/File_0700877a-931e-411b-b0c3-f612deb5e3b7?preview=1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 444\nL0@P0: PSEUDOM1ONAS AUREOFACIENS NOV. SPEC. AND ITS PIGMENTS\nL1@P0: A. J. KLUYVER*\nL2@P0: Laboratory for Mlicr-obiology, Technological Unimersity, Delft, Holland\nL3@P0: Received for publication March 12, 1956\nL4@P0: It is well knowni that the genus Pseudomonas\nL5@P0: comllpirises several p)igment-producing species,\nL6@P0: an(l that in all cases in whiich the constitution\nL7@P0: of these pigments lhais l)een estal)lishedl thle\u0002have leen found to l)e plhenlzine (lerivatives.\nL8@P0: Wrede and Strack (1929) were the first to prove\nL9@P0: this foI pyocyanine, the pigment produce(l by\nL10@P0: Pseudomonas aeruginosa (P. pyocyanea). Pyocv\u0002anine is now usually consider(ed to be a reso\u0002nance hybrid of the mesomeric forms of n-methyl\nL11@P0: 1-hydrioxyphenazine (Hillemann, 1938). K6gl\nL12@P0: and Postowsky (1930) established that chloio\u0002raphine, the green pigiment of Pseudomonas\nL13@P0: chlororaphis, is the semiquinoine of phenazine-a\u0002carboxylic acid amide, while Clemo aind Daglish\nL14@P0: (1950) proved io(linin, the pigment of Pseudo\u0002nm,onas iodina, to l)e 1:6-dihydroxyphenazine-di\u0002n-oxide.\nL15@P0: In view of these (latta it seems worthwhile to\nL16@P0: look out for further pigment-producing species\nL17@P0: within the genus Pseuidomonas in order to check\nL18@P0: if still other phenazine (lerivatives occur as\nL19@P0: natural products.' The opportunity presented\nL20@P0: itself in 1936 when MrI. H. Bouman, working\nL21@P0: in this laboratory, incidentally came across a\nL22@P0: bacterium which was easily identified as a\nL23@P0: Pseudomnonas species, and which attracted atten\u0002tion by an ample formation of yellow crystals in\nL24@P0: its colonies. Mr. Bouman showed that a crys\u0002talline pigment could also be isolated from\nL25@P0: various liquid medlia in which the bacterium\nL26@P0: had grown.\nL27@P0: Some years later his observations were ex\u0002tended, first by MrI. J. J. Ghijsen, and next by\nL28@P0: Mr. C. J. P. Spruit. They obtained convincing\nL29@P0: evridence that the pigment as first isolated by\nL30@P0: Bouman was phenazine-a-carboxy lic acid, and\nL31@P0: therefore closely related to chlororaphine. At\nL32@P0: that time war interfered with the work, and the\nL33@P0: results obtained remained unpublished.\nL34@P0: * Deceased May 1956.\nL35@P0: l In this connection the pseudomonad strain C\nL36@P0: 50 studied by Chinn (1954), which forms greenish\nL37@P0: crystals in agar media, also asks for attention.\nL38@P0: Interest in the organism in question and its\nL39@P0: products was revived a year ago when during a\nL40@P0: visit at the Noorthern Utilization Research\nL41@P0: Branch at Peorii, Ill., I)r. W. C. Haynes and\nL42@P0: Dr. F. H. Stodola informed the author regarding\nL43@P0: their investigations on a new pigment-producing\nL44@P0: pseudomonad for w-hich they also had estab\u0002lished production of phenazine-a-carboxylic\nL45@P0: acid. After exchange of cultures it soon became\nL46@P0: evident that the American and the Dutch strains\nL47@P0: were either idenitical or at least very closely\nL48@P0: rielated. The American woorkers and the present\nL49@P0: author then (leci(led on simultaneous publication\nL50@P0: of the results obtaine(l in each of the two labora\u0002tories. In the meantime there also had been an\nL51@P0: exchange of experimental data, which, of course,\nL52@P0: led to a mutual influence in the later phase of\nL53@P0: the investigation. The experimental work per\u0002formed in Delft during this second period was\nL54@P0: carried out by the following students who suc\u0002cessively devoted themselves for a short time to\nL55@P0: the problems involved: Tjoa Hin Soey, E. van\nL56@P0: der Slooten, WY. Klop, Sie Hian Jang, and E. J.\nL57@P0: Vles. The following expose should be considered\nL58@P0: as a condensedl collective report on the fragmen\u0002tary observations made over the whole period of\nL59@P0: almost twenty years; it is impossible to acknowl\u0002edge here the separate individual contributions.\nL60@P0: RESULTS\nL61@P0: Isolation and cultural properties of the organism.\nL62@P0: For reasons which can be left out of consideration\nL63@P0: here i\\h. Bouman in 1936 Imade some experiments\nL64@P0: in which kerosene was treated with clay. A liter\nL65@P0: of heat-sterilized kerosene was poured into a 2-L\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCytovirin | C17H26N8O5 | CID 25078184 - PubChem (https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Cytovirin)\nciteturn28view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Cytovirin\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 5\nL0: 【0†Image: PubChem logo】\nL1: \nL2: # JavaScript is required...\nL3: \nL4: Please enable Javascript in order to use PubChem website.\n"}
